.TH RXE38 1 "August 2026" "rxe" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rxe38 \- crack a BIP38-encrypted Bitcoin private key from a regex keyspace
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rxe38
.RI [ options ]
.I 6P...key
.RI [ passphrase-regex ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rxe38
recovers the passphrase of a BIP38-encrypted (no-EC-multiply) Bitcoin
private key by trying every passphrase in a regex-described keyspace.
It enumerates the set with librxe's enumerator \(em the same walk
.BR rxenum (1)
uses \(em and, for each candidate, runs the full BIP38 decryption and
verifies the result against the key's own address hash. On a hit it prints
the passphrase, the recovered key in WIF, and the candidate's index.
.PP
With no regex, rxe38 only parses the key and dumps its fields (compressed
flag, address hash, and the two encrypted halves). Only no-EC-multiply keys
are handled \(em those beginning
.B 6PR
or
.BR 6PY ,
base58 prefix
.BR 0x0142 .
EC-multiply keys
.RB ( 6Pf / 6Pg ,
prefix
.BR 0x0143 )
are rejected.
.PP
BIP38 is deliberately expensive: each candidate runs
.B scrypt(N=16384, r=8, p=8)
\(em memory-hard, ~16\ MB of working set \(em and then a secp256k1 point
multiply to derive the address. Throughput is therefore measured in
.I candidates per second,
not hashes per second, and the value of rxe38 is the
.I structured keyspace:
you can only crack BIP38 with a constrained guess space, such as a
half-remembered passphrase, which is exactly what a regex expresses.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-j " jobs"
Number of CPU worker threads (default 1). Shards the enumeration like
.BR rxejit (1)'s
sinks. Ignored under
.B \-G
(the GPU path shards internally).
.TP
.B \-G
Run scrypt on the GPU (OpenCL) and finish the verify on the host. This is
the fast path; see
.B THE GPU BACKEND
below.
.TP
.BI \-\-backend " name"
Select the GPU backend:
.B opencl
(the default, portable, the champion \(em also the only backend that runs on
non-NVIDIA GPUs) or
.B cuda
(reserved for a future NVIDIA-only fast path; not built in by default).
May also be set with the
.B RXE38_BACKEND
environment variable.
.TP
.BI \-b " lanes"
GPU batch size, in lanes (candidates resident on the device at once). Each
lane needs about 16\ MB of scratchpad, so this is bounded by device memory.
0 or omitted auto-fills a fraction of VRAM.
.TP
.BI \-c " count"
Try at most
.I count
candidates, then stop (0 = no limit).
.TP
.B \-p
Print a progress line (rate and ETA) while cracking.
.SH THE BIP38 PIPELINE
For a no-EC-multiply key, rxe38 performs, per candidate:
.PP
.RS
.nf
base58check-decode -> [0x0142] flag addrhash(4) enc1(16) enc2(16)
d   = scrypt(passphrase, salt=addrhash, 16384, 8, 8, 64)
dh1 = d[0:32];  dh2 = d[32:64]
priv = AES256_ECB_dec(enc1, dh2) XOR dh1[0:16]
     || AES256_ECB_dec(enc2, dh2) XOR dh1[16:32]
VERIFY: SHA256d(base58_address(priv, compressed))[0:4] == addrhash
.fi
.RE
.PP
"No EC multiply" describes how the key was
.I encrypted;
it does
.B not
mean the address check is skipped. The 4-byte address hash is the only
verifier in the key, so deriving the address \(em which requires the
secp256k1 multiply \(em is mandatory. Without it every passphrase would
appear to "succeed".
.PP
Every component (base58check, scrypt, AES-256, secp256k1, RIPEMD-160) is
verified byte-exact against
.B rxe38/oracle.py
and the RFC 7914 / FIPS-197 test vectors; see
.BR "SELF-TESTS" .
.SH THE GPU BACKEND
Under
.BR \-G ,
scrypt \(em about 99.99% of the per-candidate cost \(em runs on the GPU
while the host finishes AES, secp256k1, and the address verify (threaded,
overlapped with the next GPU batch). The OpenCL kernel lives in
.B rxe38/scrypt.cl
and is embedded into the binary at build time.
.PP
The scrypt block/scratchpad is laid out
.I lane-interleaved
(word
.I w
of lane
.I g
at index
.IR w*NLANES+g )
so a warp's reads coalesce, and the working block is handled as
.B uint4
vectors to cut load/store traffic. Two kernels are provided: a
.I phased
kernel that runs ROMix in short, watchdog-safe launches (the default, so
rxe38 works on a GPU that also drives a display), and a single-launch
.I monolithic
kernel that is faster but must run on a headless card
.RB ( RXE38_GPU_MONO=1 ).
.PP
Two knobs matter most for throughput, both environment variables:
.B RXE38_GPU_GAP
(the scrypt time-memory trade-off \(em store V every
.IR g -th
block and recompute the rest, fitting ~\c
.IR g x
more lanes; \fIg\fR=3 was fastest on a 32\ GB card) and
.B RXE38_GPU_WG
(the work-group size; 32 = one warp is best here). Hit recording across the
overlapped batches is lowest-global-index-wins, so a reported hit is the
true first match in enumeration order regardless of which batch finishes
verifying first.
.SH PERFORMANCE
scrypt(16384, 8) mandates a 1\ KB working block that BlockMix must churn
2\fIN\fR times per pass. It spills out of registers into local memory, and
that local-memory bandwidth is the binding constraint \(em not compute, not
global-memory latency. On an RTX 4080 SUPER the OpenCL backend reaches
about
.B 525 candidates/second
(headless,
.BR "RXE38_GPU_MONO=1 RXE38_GPU_GAP=3" ),
roughly 20x a 22-thread CPU. This is at or near the card's practical
ceiling: three alternative kernel structures (warp-cooperative,
candidate-interleaved, all-shared) were each measured slower, and moving the
block to on-chip shared memory bought only ~9%.
.PP
For calibration:
.BR hashcat (1)
mode 8900 (generic scrypt) benchmarks about 4251\ H/s at
.IR N =16384
on a 4080 \(em but that is
.IR p =1.
BIP38 is
.IR p =8,
eight times the work per candidate, so the comparable figure is ~530
candidates/second: rxe38 is at parity with hashcat's scrypt kernel. The
gap to hashcat's headline number is entirely the
.IR p =8
work factor, not kernel efficiency. More throughput comes from more or
larger GPUs (the ceiling scales with memory bandwidth), not from a cleverer
kernel.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RXE38_BACKEND
GPU backend, same as
.BR \-\-backend .
.TP
.B RXE38_GPU_GAP
scrypt TMTO ratio: store V every
.IR g -th
block, recompute misses. \fIg\fR=1 is plain ROMix (byte-identical);
\fIg\fR=3 was fastest on a 4080.
.TP
.B RXE38_GPU_WG
OpenCL work-group size (default 32; 0 = driver's choice).
.TP
.B RXE38_GPU_MONO
If set, use the single-launch monolithic kernel (fastest, headless only \(em
no watchdog chunking).
.TP
.B RXE38_GPU_CHUNK
ROMix iterations per launch for the phased kernel. Larger amortizes the
working-block load/store; raise it on a headless card.
.TP
.B RXE38_GPU_DBLBUF
Set to 0 to disable double-buffering (overlap of a batch's verify with the
next batch's GPU run). Default on.
.TP
.B RXE38_VERIFY_THREADS
Host-side verify workers (default min(cores, 16)).
.TP
.B RXE38_GPU_N
Override scrypt
.I N
(for testing at a smaller, watchdog-safe or faster parameter).
.SH SELF-TESTS
.B rxe38
built without the GPU backend accepts three self-test modes that check the
CPU implementation against known-answer vectors:
.BR \-\-test\-scrypt
(RFC 7914 plus a BIP38 spec vector),
.BR \-\-test\-priv
(FIPS-197 AES-256 plus the recovered private keys), and
.BR \-\-test\-verify
(the full end-to-end pipeline on all four BIP38 spec vectors, plus a
wrong-passphrase negative). The GPU build adds
.BR \-\-gpu\-scrypt\-test ,
.BR \-\-gpu\-phase\-test ,
and
.B \-\-coop\-test
which diff the device kernels byte-exact against the CPU reference.
Run them with
.BR "make rxe38\-test" .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The passphrase was found (or a self-test / parse-only run succeeded).
.TP
.B 1
Not found, or a bad key.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Dump a key's fields:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rxe38 6PRVWUbkzzsbcVac2qwfssoUJAN1Xhrg6bNk8J7Nzm5H7kxEbn2Nh2ZoGg
.fi
.RE
.PP
Crack on the CPU across a small keyspace, 8 threads:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rxe38 -j8 6PRNFFkZ...  '(Alice|Bob|Satoshi|Carol)'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Crack on the GPU with progress, a half-remembered passphrase:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rxe38 -G -p 6PRVWUbk...  'S[a4]toshi20[0-2][0-9]'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Fastest on a headless card:
.PP
.RS
.nf
RXE38_GPU_MONO=1 RXE38_GPU_GAP=3 rxe38 -G 6PRVWUbk...  '<regex>'
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rxenum (1),
.BR rxejit (1),
.BR hashcat (1).
.PP
BIP 38 (Passphrase-protected private key); RFC 7914 (scrypt); FIPS-197
(AES).
